{"question":"What does the charge of being most unjust mean?","words":["وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"No one surpasses the offender in injustice; the question condemns the obstruction.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī, al-Zajjāj and later grammarians parse man as subject and aẓlam as predicate. Elmalılı defines injustice as misplacing what is sacred; Ibn ʿĀshūr says the offenders violated worshippers’ rights.","rows":["ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r1","NAHHAS:v1p256/r12","THALABI:v1p260#2/r5","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r2","JISHUMI:v1p558/r1","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r1","TABRISI:2:114#2/r2","TABRISI:2:114/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r1","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p594/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r4","BURSEVI:v1p166/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r6","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r2","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r3","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r16","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r2","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r3","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Several kinds of unbelieving offenders can tie at the highest degree of injustice.","reasons":"Abū Ḥayyān rejects restricting the phrase to classes: saying none is more unjust allows equals. Al-Ālūsī compares three equally learned people; the shared unbelief explains the tie.","rows":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r4","SAMIN-DURR:2:114/r5","BURHAN:v4p74/r3","BURHAN:v4p74/r4","ITQAN:v3p79/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r11","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r12","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r2","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r4"],"prefer":["ITQAN:v3p79/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r10"],"against":["ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r9"]},{"position":"Each superlative is limited to offenders of its own kind.","reasons":"Several grammarians, al-Burhān and al-Itqān use the relative clause to limit comparison to mosque blockers, forgers, or rejecters.","rows":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114/r3","BURHAN:v4p74/r1","BURHAN:v4p76/r1","BURHAN:v4p76/r2","BURHAN:v4p76/r3","ITQAN:v3p79/r2","ITQAN:v3p79/r3","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r3","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r9"]},{"position":"The earliest perpetrators are unsurpassed because they introduced the offense.","reasons":"Reported by Abū Ḥayyān, al-Samīn, al-Burhān and al-Itqān; al-Itqān says this reduces to restriction by offense.","rows":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r2","SAMIN-DURR:2:114/r4","BURHAN:v4p74/r2","ITQAN:v3p79/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p572/r3"]},{"position":"The superlative is figurative hyperbole expressing horror at the act.","reasons":"Al-Zarkashī prefers this to a literal ranking; al-Ālūsī accepts it as an alternative.","rows":["BURHAN:v4p76/r4","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r2"],"prefer":["BURHAN:v4p76/r4"],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether the superlative is literal, restricted by class, or rhetorical."}
{"question":"Who are the mosque preventers and what event occasioned the verse?","words":["مِمَّن مَّنَعَ مَسَٰجِدَ ٱللَّهِ"],"type":"reports","positions":[{"position":"Christians or Roman Christians desecrated and obstructed worship at Jerusalem.","reasons":"Mujāhid reports dumped filth; Ibn ʿAbbās and others identify Christians. Reports name Titus and Roman forces, killing and enslaving Israelites, burning Torah, desecrating the Temple; many say it remained ruined until ʿUmar. Elmalılı details Titus, Hadrian, Helena and eventual Muslim rebuilding. Al-Ṭabarī prefers this due to physical ruin and context; Abū Bakr al-Rāzī and Kuran Yolu object that Christians postdate Nebuchadnezzar and revered the site.","rows":["MUJAHID:v1p86/r1","MUQATIL:2:114/r1","MUQATIL:2:114/r3","MUQATIL:2:114/r4","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p61#2/r1","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r3","TAB-FULL:v2p444/r3","ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r2","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r6","THALABI:v1p260#2/r1","THALABI:v1p260#2/r6","WAHIDI-ASBAB:v1p36/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r3","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:2:113-114/r6","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:2:113-114/r7","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r9","JISHUMI:v1p558/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r1","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p204#2/r12","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r2","TABRISI:2:114/r13","TABRISI:2:114/r14","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r2","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v1p133#4/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p9/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r7","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r12","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r2","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r8","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r10","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r12","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r8","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r4","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r2","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r3","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r8","BURSEVI:v1p166/r3","BURSEVI:v1p166/r4","BURSEVI:v1p166/r6","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r3","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r6","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r9","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r12","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r13","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r14","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r15","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r16","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r17","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r18","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r19","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r1","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r2","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r5","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r5","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r6","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r7","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r3","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r5"],"prefer":["TAB-FULL:v2p444/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r8"],"against":[]},{"position":"Meccan polytheists, especially Quraysh, barred the Prophet and Muslims from the Sacred Mosque.","reasons":"Reports locate the bar at the Kaaba or al-Ḥudaybiyya, where sacrifice at Dhū Ṭuwā and a truce followed; others mention expulsion, Abu Bakr's prayer place, and harassment of the Prophet. Ibn Kathīr and Ibn ʿĀshūr prefer this in context. Al-Ṭabarī objects that Quraysh did not demolish the Kaaba; Ibn Kathīr replies that idolatry and exclusion ruined it in substance.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r7","TAB-FULL:v2p444/r1","ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r1","THALABI:v1p261/r6","WAHIDI-ASBAB:v1p36/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r4","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r11","JISHUMI:v1p558/r3","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r15","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p204#2/r13","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r5","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r3","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r7","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v1p133#4/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r14","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r9","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r14","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r5","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r7","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r3","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r1","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r9","SUYUTI-LUBAB:v1p25/r2","SUYUTI-LUBAB:v1p25/r3","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r2","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r3","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r4","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r7","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r8","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r2","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r6","TABATABAI:2:108-115/r10","TABATABAI:2:108-115/r11","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r8","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r9","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r4","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r7","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r8"],"prefer":["IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r6","TABATABAI:2:108-115/r10"],"against":["TAB-FULL:v2p444/r3","TABRISI:2:114/r13","TABRISI:2:114/r14"]},{"position":"The immediate historical cases differ, but the wording includes every person and every mosque.","reasons":"Al-Qurṭubī and Abū Ḥayyān prefer the general wording. Al-Zajjāj cites the earth made a mosque; al-Kashshāf and al-Nasafī compare 104:1, a general rebuke from a particular occasion. Elmalılı includes Jews, Christians and polytheists; Asad and Kuran Yolu extend protection to houses of worship.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p446/r1","ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r5","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p252/r4","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r12","JISHUMI:v1p558/r4","JISHUMI:v1p559/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p204#2/r11","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r1","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r3","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r6","TABRISI:2:114#2/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r10","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r1","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r7","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r4","BURSEVI:v1p166/r6","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r5","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r5","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r3","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r8","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r6","ASAD-NOTES:2:114#n95/r1","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r12","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r13","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r14","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r6"],"prefer":["QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r10","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r1","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r8"],"against":[]},{"position":"Nebuchadnezzar and his followers ruined Jerusalem, aided by Romans or Christians.","reasons":"Qatāda, al-Suddī and others say hostility to Jews, sometimes after the killing of John son of Zechariah, prompted the alliance; variants name Jews who induced the attack. Abū Bakr al-Rāzī objects to the chronology. Ibn ʿĀshūr reports Nebuchadnezzar's three campaigns but rejects this occasion on contextual grounds.","rows":["ABDURRAZZAQ:v1p56/r1","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r4","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r5","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r6","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r7","THALABI:v1p260#2/r2","THALABI:v1p260#2/r3","THALABI:v1p260#2/r4","WAHIDI-ASBAB:v1p36/r2","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r10","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r3","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r4","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r3","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r6","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p9/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r11","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r12","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p570/r13","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r4","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r4","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r7","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r4","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":["RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r9","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r6","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r5"]},{"position":"Jews after the qibla change sought to obstruct prayer toward the Kaaba and perhaps stirred attacks on it and the Prophet's Mosque.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī prefers this because the nearby verses censure Jews and Christians; Kuran Yolu reports his fifth view.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r9","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r11"],"prefer":["RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r8"],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Historical chronology, the context of 2:113, and whether ruin was physical or through obstructed worship."}
{"question":"What does منع mean and what obstruction counts here?","words":["مَّنَعَ"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"It means interposing between a willing person and worship or access.","reasons":"Jishumī and al-Ṭabrisī compare ṣadd; Abū Ḥayyān cites al-Rāghib's protective sense. Ibn ʿĀshūr specifies denying qualified worshippers appointed rites, while ordinary closure and exclusion of unqualified entrants are outside the charge.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r1","TABRISI:2:114/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r8","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What are God's masājid, and why is the plural used?","words":["مَسَٰجِدَ ٱللَّهِ"],"type":"referent","positions":[{"position":"Every mosque or place of worship is within the wording.","reasons":"A mosque is a place of prostration, and the earth was made a mosque and purifier. Al-Kashshāf, al-Qurṭubī, Abū Ḥayyān and others distinguish the general rule from a specific occasion. Ibn ʿAṭiyya includes a Muslim city because the whole earth is a mosque.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p441/r1","ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r5","JISHUMI:v1p558/r4","TABRISI:2:114#2/r3","TABRISI:2:114#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r6","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r10","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r5","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r5","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r6","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r1","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r12","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Jerusalem's Temple and its prayer niches.","reasons":"Muqātil, al-Ṭabarī's preferred context, al-Dāmghānī and others identify the sanctuary. The plural may include its many prayer spaces.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p444/r3","THALABI:v1p260#2/r6","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r3","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:2:113-114/r6","DAMGHANI:v1p231/r3","JISHUMI:v1p558/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r6","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r3","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r2","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Masjid literally means a place of prostration, conventionally a building for prayer.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī gives singular/plural morphology, Ibn al-Jawzī and Kuran Yolu the technical use; Abū Ḥayyān says naming prostration reflects full submission and cites nearness to God in prostration. Turkish terms jāmiʿ, musallā and namāzgāh are distinguished.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p441/r1","TAB-FULL:v2p441/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p441/r3","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r3","TABRISI:2:114/r4","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r5","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r1","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r3","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r4","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The Sacred Mosque in Mecca, or it and other Meccan worship places.","reasons":"Abū Muslim makes one mosque plural; al-Rāzī and Ibn ʿĀshūr mention Abu Bakr's mosque, the Kaaba, Maqām Ibrāhīm, Ḥaṭīm, Khayf, Minā and Mashʿar. Others say the Kaaba is qibla of all mosques or the plural magnifies it.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p558/r3","JISHUMI:v1p558/r5","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r3","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r7","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r2","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r4","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p679/r5","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r3","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether a particular sanctuary, all mosques, or the earth is intended; why a plural may refer to a single case."}
{"question":"How does the clause 'that His name be mentioned' attach to 'prevented'?","words":["أَن يُذْكَرَ فِيهَا ٱسْمُهُۥ"],"type":"grammar","positions":[{"position":"A preposition, usually min, is understood before it.","reasons":"Al-Akhfash and others allow an omitted preposition, with accusative or genitive analyses; al-Naḥḥās notes length or the verb's sense licenses deletion.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p442/r1","NAHHAS:v1p256/r13","THALABI:v1p260#2/r7","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r6","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r8","TABRISI:2:114/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r9","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r6","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r2","BURSEVI:v1p166/r7"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is the second object of 'prevented'.","reasons":"Al-Kashshāf compares 17:59 and 17:94; al-Wāḥidī calls it a verbal-noun clause. Some allow direct double-object government.","rows":["THALABI:v1p260#2/r7","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p254/r2","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r8","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p204#2/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r8","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r4","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r2","BURSEVI:v1p166/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is an apposition to 'mosques', particularly an inclusive apposition.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī, al-Zajjāj, al-Ṭabrisī and al-Samīn state this analysis.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p442/r2","ZAJJAJ:v1p195#2/r2","NAHHAS:v1p256/r13","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r7","TABRISI:2:114/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r10","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It expresses the purpose or motive of prevention: lest God be remembered.","reasons":"Al-Zajjāj makes dislike of God's name its governing motive; al-Samīn says an omitted noun such as 'entry' is needed.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r8","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p204#2/r10","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r8","TABRISI:2:114/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r11","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r4","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether it is a second object, apposition, purpose clause or governed by an omitted preposition."}
{"question":"What does mentioning God's name there encompass?","words":["يُذْكَرَ فِيهَا ٱسْمُهُۥ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"It encompasses monotheism, prayer, recitation and inner and outward worship.","reasons":"Muqātil specifies affirming oneness; Abū Ḥayyān says even the easiest act of remembrance was barred. Bursevī adds glorification and sanctification.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r2","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p9#2/r6","BURSEVI:v1p166/r8","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r4","TABRISI:2:114#2/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What does saʿā mean and how does it work here?","words":["وَسَعَىٰ"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"It is purposeful effort or action toward an end.","reasons":"Al-Wāḥidī traces quick walking to walking then work, citing 28:20, 37:102, 62:9, 2:260, 17:19, 22:51 and 92:4. Jishumī, al-Ṭabrisī, Abū Ḥayyān and Ibn ʿĀshūr give walking, seeking, earning and causation; poetry is cited for livelihood and alms. It is coordinated with manaʿa and fī kharābihā attaches to it.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p442#2/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r6","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r7","TABRISI:2:114/r2","TABRISI:2:114/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r6","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r1","BURSEVI:v1p166/r9","NAHHAS:v1p256/r14","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r7"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What does kharāb mean here and how is it caused?","words":["فِى خَرَابِهَآ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"Preventing prayer and remembrance is itself figurative ruin.","reasons":"Ibn Zayd says barring pilgrims and maintainers empties the mosque; al-Rāzī and Ibn Kathīr say idolatry, expulsion and inactivity undo its purpose. Ibn ʿĀshūr distinguishes literal blocking from metaphorical ruin in the Meccan account.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p444/r2","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p250#2/r5","JISHUMI:v1p558/r6","JISHUMI:v1p558/r7","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r16","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r2","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r9","TABRISI:2:114#3/r1","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v1p133#4/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r13","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r14","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r11","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r5","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r4","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p387#2/r6","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r4","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r4","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r5","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r2","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r7","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is literal demolition and desecration of a sanctuary.","reasons":"Jerusalem accounts name wrecking, burned Torah, corpses and pigs. Jishumī and Abū Ḥayyān define kharāb against construction; al-Samīn gives verbal-noun analyses, including object and subject annexation.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r3","MUQATIL:2:114/r4","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r4","THALABI:v1p260#2/r8","TABRISI:2:114/r4","TABRISI:2:114/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r12","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r11","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p568/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p569/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r3","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r8","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#2/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r5","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r10","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r11","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r12","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r13","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r14","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r15","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r16","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r17","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r18","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r19"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether the ruin is physical destruction, loss of worship, or both."}
{"question":"How is the entry clause parsed and why does it shift to a plural?","words":["أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ مَا كَانَ لَهُمْ أَن يَدْخُلُوهَآ إِلَّا خَآئِفِينَ"],"type":"grammar","positions":[{"position":"The demonstrative is subject; the entry clause is its predicate, and khāʾifīn is a circumstantial accusative.","reasons":"Al-Samīn makes lahum the fronted predicate of kāna and an yadkhulūhā its subject; al-Ṭabrisī also allows kāna as redundant. The exception excludes all states except fear. The singular form of man governs earlier verbs and its collective sense governs the plural.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r6","NAHHAS:v1p256/r15","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r12","TABRISI:2:114/r10","TABRISI:2:114/r11","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r8","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r9","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#3/r1","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#3/r2","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#3/r4","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r4","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"How is the fearful-entry phrase read?","words":["إِلَّا خَآئِفِينَ"],"type":"readings","positions":[{"position":"Ubayy's codex reads khīfāʾ or khīfan, a plural conveying fear.","reasons":"Abū Ḥayyān and al-Samīn compare ṣawm/ṣaym and explain wāw-to-yāʾ change.","rows":["THALABI:v1p260#2/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r8","SAMIN-DURR:2:114#3/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The standard wording is khāʾifīn.","reasons":"Ibn al-Jazarī rejects a reported shortest madd on ulāʾika: no imam adopts it and the following kasra agrees with khāʾifīn. Reciters differ in madd length and hamza pause treatment; Sībawayh's hamza rules explain easing and replacement.","rows":["NASHR:v1p317/r1","NASHR:v1p317/r2","NASHR:v1p317/r3","NASHR:v1p317/r4","NASHR:v1p353/r1","NASHR:v1p353/r2","NASHR:v1p353/r3","NASHR:v1p353/r4","NASHR:v1p353/r5","NASHR:v1p461/r1","NASHR:v1p461/r2","NASHR:v1p462/r1","NASHR:v1p462/r2","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f1","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f2","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f3","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f4","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f5","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f6","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f7","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f8","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f10","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f11","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f12","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f13","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f14","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f15","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f16","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f21","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f22","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f23","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f24","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f25","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f26","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f27","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f28","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f29","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f30","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f31","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f32","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f33","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f34","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f35","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f36","SIBAWAYH:(هذابابالهمز)#649/f37"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Abū ʿAbd Allāh reads khīfan.","reasons":"Al-Kashshāf compares its form with ṣiyam.","rows":["KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r10"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"A reading attributed to ʿAbd Allāh gives 'except ḥanīfan'.","reasons":"","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r17"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Variant forms and the reciters' arguments about hamza and vowel length."}
{"question":"What does the fearful-entry clause mean?","words":["مَا كَانَ لَهُمْ أَن يَدْخُلُوهَآ إِلَّا خَآئِفِينَ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"Christians or Romans enter Jerusalem secretly fearing beating, execution or jizya.","reasons":"Qatāda, al-Suddī, Ibn ʿAbbās and others describe disguise, severe beating, death or poll tax after the Muslim rebuilding. Al-Rāzī objects that Christians later held Jerusalem for more than a century; al-Ālūsī says one period suffices for the promise.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r5","ABDURRAZZAQ:v1p56/r2","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p61#2/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r3","TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r6","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r7","IBNABIHATIM:v1p211/r1","THALABI:v1p260#2/r10","THALABI:v1p260#2/r11","THALABI:v1p260#2/r12","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p252/r1","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:2:113-114/r8","JISHUMI:v1p558/r8","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r8","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r9","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r10","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r7","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r8","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r10","TABRISI:2:114#3/r2","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v1p133#4/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r7","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r10","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r10","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r11","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r12","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r3","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r5","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r8","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r11","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r12","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r4","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r5","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It predicts Muslim victory, recovery of mosques and their fearful entry.","reasons":"Al-Zajjāj links 9:33; al-Rāzī, Ibn Kathīr and Ibn ʿĀshūr cite the conquest, ban on polytheist pilgrimage and expulsion. Al-Ālūsī says Crusader control later does not negate a promise fulfilled once. Abū Ḥayyān objects that mā kāna lahum expresses propriety, not plain prediction.","rows":["ZAJJAJ:v1p196/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p254/r1","JISHUMI:v1p558/r11","TABRISI:2:114#3/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r6","THALABI:v1p261/r7","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r17","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r4","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r8","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389/r4","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r12","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r13","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p680/r5","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p681/r1","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r5","TABATABAI:2:108-115/r5"],"prefer":["ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r13"],"against":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r2"]},{"position":"The declarative form commands Muslims to bar or expel the offenders.","reasons":"Explanations compare 33:53; some demand jihad until fearful entry, others forbid enabling access. Ibn Kathīr specifies truce and jizya. Abū Ḥayyān finds this strained.","rows":["THALABI:v1p260#2/r13","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p252/r3","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r11","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r12","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v1p133#4/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r8","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r2","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r9","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r7","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r13"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They ought to fear believers' punishment and must not seize mosques.","reasons":"Al-Kashshāf and al-Nasafī say oppression prevented this rightful arrangement; al-Ālūsī reads lām as entitlement violated.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r1","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r4","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r5","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r8","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r4","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r17"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The offenders ought to enter reverently, fearing God and His punishment.","reasons":"Abū Ḥayyān prefers the mosque's dignity as the direct force of mā kāna lahum and rejects a simple future prediction and command. Al-Ālūsī says the lām marks fitting entitlement; Ibn ʿĀshūr rejects this because the demonstrative makes the condition a consequence of offense.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r13","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r14","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r7","BURSEVI:v1p166/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r7","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p681/r2"],"prefer":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p573#2/r13"],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether it states rightful reverence, predicts reversal of power, or commands exclusion."}
{"question":"What access to mosques may non-Muslims have?","words":["أَن يَدْخُلُوهَآ"],"type":"law","positions":[{"position":"They may enter no mosque, apart from reported necessity or judicial exceptions.","reasons":"Mālik invokes 9:28 and purification from impurity. Al-Jubbāʾī allows a litigant brief fearful entry to ordinary mosques; Abū ʿAlī gives judicial authority. Al-Qurṭubī reads fear as expulsion or discipline.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p558/r9","JISHUMI:v1p559/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r11","TABRISI:2:114#3/r4","TABRISI:2:114#3/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r14","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r9","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r15"],"prefer":["TABRISI:2:114#3/r5"],"against":[]},{"position":"The Sacred Mosque is barred to them; ordinary mosques are distinguished.","reasons":"Al-Shāfiʿī cites 9:28, 22:26, 9:17–18 and 2:114, with the sanctuary's special sanctity. Al-Rāzī rejects narrowing 9:28 to pilgrimage and notes 17:1 can use Sacred Mosque for the whole sanctuary.","rows":["BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r18","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r11","TABRISI:2:114#3/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p10#2/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r13","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r16","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r17","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r16"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They may enter mosques generally.","reasons":"Abū Ḥanīfa cites this verse, lodging of the Thaqīf delegation and sanctuary safety. Al-Rāzī says the historical reports predate 9:28 and rejects analogy from ordinary mosques to the Sacred Mosque.","rows":["KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r15","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r16","RAZI-FULL:v4p16/r17","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r14"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The verse itself does not settle the legal entry rule.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭiyya says it points toward but does not explicitly prohibit entry; Abū Ḥayyān says his reverence reading cannot prove exclusion or fearful entry permission. Asad and Kuran Yolu cite the Christian Najrān delegation worshipping in the Prophet's mosque.","rows":["IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r13","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p574/r10","ASAD-NOTES:2:114#n95/r1","ASAD-NOTES:2:114#n95/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r15"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"The sanctity of the Sacred Mosque, scope of 9:28, reports of actual entry, and whether 2:114 proves exclusion."}
{"question":"What does khizy mean and what is their worldly disgrace?","words":["لَهُمْ فِى ٱلدُّنْيَا خِزْىٌۭ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"Belligerents face killing or captivity; protected non-Muslims pay jizya in humiliation.","reasons":"Qatāda, al-Zajjāj and many others distinguish the two legal statuses. Al-Ṭabarī includes both in worldly abasement.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r6","ABDURRAZZAQ:v1p56/r3","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r1","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r2","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r4","ZAJJAJ:v1p196/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v1p211/r3","THALABI:v1p261/r1","MAWARDI-FULL:v1p174/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p254/r3","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:2:113-114/r9","JISHUMI:v1p558/r12","JISHUMI:v1p559/r1","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r12","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r19","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p206/r1","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199#2/r1","TABRISI:2:114#3/r7","TABRISI:2:114#3/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p11/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r10","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r13","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r5","BURSEVI:v1p166/r11","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r18","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r11","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Their cities will be conquered, especially Constantinople, Rome and Amorium.","reasons":"Muqātil and al-Kalbī list the cities; al-Suddī and others connect conquest to the Mahdī, killing and captivity. Al-Farrāʾ places it at the end of time, while Jishumī rejects that as against the apparent sense. Elmalılı treats Constantinople's later fall as a fulfilled prophecy.","rows":["IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p61#2/r4","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v1p211/r2","THALABI:v1p261/r2","THALABI:v1p261/r3","THALABI:v1p261/r5","MAWARDI-FULL:v1p174/r2","JISHUMI:v1p558/r13","JISHUMI:v1p558/r14","JISHUMI:v1p559/r4","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r13","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p206/r2","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199#2/r2","TABRISI:2:114#3/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r10","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r5","DURR-FULL:v1p264/r6","BURSEVI:v1p166/r12","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r7","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Khizy is disgrace or abasement, broadly covering punitive loss of power.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī and al-Rāzī include killing, captivity and jizya; Ibn Kathīr says the sense exceeds specific glosses. Elmalılı describes loss of state and strength. Ibn ʿĀshūr identifies Badr deaths, captivity and flight at the conquest.","rows":["IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p61#2/r3","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r1","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r4","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p11/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389#2/r7","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r6","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p682/r1","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r10"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"For Quraysh it is defeat, killing, captivity and expulsion at the conquest.","reasons":"This reading distinguishes those who died unbelievers for afterlife punishment; al-Ālūsī adds destroyed idols and leaders' deaths.","rows":["THALABI:v1p261/r9","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199#2/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r18","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r9","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p682/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Their disgrace is being excluded from mosques or the Sacred Mosque.","reasons":"Abū ʿAlī and others take denied entry as public abasement; Ibn Kathīr frames expulsion as repayment for barring believers.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p559/r2","TABRISI:2:114#3/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p11/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r8","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether disgrace takes the form of jizya, war losses, exclusion, conquests, or a wider humiliation."}
{"question":"What is the great punishment in the hereafter, and why is it great?","words":["وَلَهُمْ فِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ عَذَابٌ عَظِيمٌۭ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"It is Hellfire, commensurate with unbelief and grave wrongdoing.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī, al-Thaʿlabī and many others identify Hell; al-Zajjāj, al-Rāzī and al-Ālūsī link greatness to the opening accusation. Abū Ḥayyān says its degree distinguishes unbelievers from believers and its fire mirrors their demolition; Ibn Kathīr cites desecration with idols and naked circumambulation.","rows":["MUQATIL:2:114/r6","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r3","TAB-FULL:v2p448/r4","ZAJJAJ:v1p196/r4","THALABI:v1p261/r4","MAWARDI-FULL:v1p174/r3","JISHUMI:v1p559/r3","BAGHAWI-FULL:v1p138/r14","TABRISI:2:114#3/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p11/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r5","BAYDAWI-FULL:2:114/r11","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r14","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p389/r6","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r19","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r9","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p682/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"How does this verse connect with its neighbors and other verses?","words":["*"],"type":"links","positions":[{"position":"It follows the rebuke of Jews and Christians by exposing Christian offenses.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī cites 2:113 and 2:116; al-Rāzī says Christian destruction contradicts their Paradise claim; al-Ālūsī makes this the first occasion's link.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v2p444/r3","JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r13","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r3","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It turns from Jews and Christians to Arab polytheists with analogous conduct.","reasons":"Jishumī, al-Rāzī, Ibn Kathīr and Ibn ʿĀshūr find the previous mention of those without knowledge or a digression. Ibn ʿĀshūr links the qibla dispute: exclusion from the sanctuary was graver than facing another direction.","rows":["JISHUMI:v1p556#2/r14","TABRISI:2:114#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p10/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v1p388/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363/r4","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p678#2/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p682/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The passage is linked to other mosque and worship verses.","reasons":"The sources cite 9:17–18 on mosque maintenance, 9:28 on barring polytheists, 22:40 on protection of all houses of worship and 72:18 on mosques belonging to God.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r10","ASAD-NOTES:2:114#n95/r1","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r14","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r12"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Verse 2:115 consoles those denied Jerusalem or a mosque: east and west belong to God.","reasons":"Al-Dahhān's explanation appears in al-Burhān and al-Itqān.","rows":["BURHAN:v1p44/r7","ITQAN:v3p295/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The next statement about God taking a son connects to the mosque-preventer passage.","reasons":"Al-Wāḥidī uses the Syrian codex reading of Ibn ʿĀmir without the waw before qālū to mark the connection.","rows":["BURHAN:v3p211/r1","ELMALILI:2:114-115#2/r21"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"The proposed historical addressee and how the passage turns to 2:115."}
{"question":"What inward or spiritual analogy do the sources draw?","words":["*"],"type":"inward","positions":[{"position":"Mosques may analogize hearts, souls, spirits and inner secrets; ruin is desire and veiling.","reasons":"Al-Qushayrī distinguishes ruined homes of worship, gnosis, love and witnessing. Qāshānī and al-Ālūsī read divine names manifesting according to the heart, desire and fanaticism clouding it, humbled entry, worldly exposure and afterlife veiling. Ibn ʿĀshūr frames this as analogy, not a substitute exegesis; Abū Ḥayyān rejects it as unsupported.","rows":["QUSHAYRI:v1p62/r1","QUSHAYRI:v1p62/r2","QUSHAYRI:v1p62/r3","QUSHAYRI:v1p62/r4","QUSHAYRI:v1p62/r5","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r1","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r2","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r3","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r4","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r5","QASHANI:2:113-114#2/r6","QASHANI:2:113-114/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r11","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p575/r12","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r21","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r22","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r23","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r24","ALUSI-FULL:v1p363#2/r25","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r2","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r5","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r6","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r7","IBNASHUR-FULL:v1p34/r8","STUDYQURAN:2:114/r11"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether inward allusion is legitimate and whether it replaces the outward meaning."}
{"question":"What does this verse teach about honoring and maintaining worship places?","words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"Preventing worship is grave; maintaining worship places includes use, repair and protecting worshippers.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī cites 72:18, 9:18, 24:36 and reports on mosque building, attendance and prayer rewards. Al-Qurṭubī stresses prayer's merit, mosque public status and protection against demolition. Asad and Kuran Yolu extend respect to churches and synagogues per 22:40.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p11#2/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p12/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p13/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p13#2/r10","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r5","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r6","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#2/r7","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r5","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r6","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r7","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r8","BURSEVI:v1p166#2/r9","ASAD-NOTES:2:114#n95/r1","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r12","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r13","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:2:114/r14","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r1","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r2","ELMALILI:2:114-115/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What conduct and activities in mosques do the cited reports discuss?","words":["*"],"type":"law","positions":[{"position":"Mosques are for prayer, remembrance, cleanliness and dignified use.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī collects reports against trade, poetry, lost-property calls, spitting and odors; records greetings, sleeping, reclining, building without distracting decoration, and differences over greeting prayer and adjudication. The Bedouin urination report requires cleaning with water and teaching the mosque's purpose.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p14/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r13","RAZI-FULL:v4p14#2/r14","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r1","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r2","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r3","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r4","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r5","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r6","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r7","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r8","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r9","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r10","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r11","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r13","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r14","RAZI-FULL:v4p15/r15"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Different rulings on entry prayer, sleeping, transactions, adjudication and upkeep."}
{"question":"What other cited reports or scholarly discussions accompany the verse?","words":["*"],"type":"reports","positions":[{"position":"Ancillary reports and methodological discussions appear alongside the exegesis.","reasons":"The Musnad report describes ʿAlī's ablution and his ranking of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. Ibn al-Jawzī surveys masjid referents elsewhere. Bursevī cites a Mathnawī poem about confusing shadow and source. Rifāʿī surveys the controversy over Khalafallāh, symbolic Qurʾānic narrative, and responses from al-Khūlī, Muḥammad ʿAbduh and critics. Further cited details occur in the remaining notes (r5, r2, r5, r16, r8, r2, r10, r9, r4, r7, r11, r3, r12, r1, r1, r4, r12, r2, r5, r6, r3, r5, r11, r1, r6, r2, r1, r5, r2, r6, r10, r6, r20, r10, r7, r10, r18).","rows":["MUSNAD-AHMAD:1050/r1","MUSNAD-AHMAD:1050/r2","MUSNAD-AHMAD:1050/r3","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r4","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r5","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r6","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r8","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p567/r9","BURSEVI:v1p166/r1","BURSEVI:v1p166/r2","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f1","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f2","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f3","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f4","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f5","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f6","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f7","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f8","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f9","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f10","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f11","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f12","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f13","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f14","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f15","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f16","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f17","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f18","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f19","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f20","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f21","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f22","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f23","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f24","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f25","RIFAI-KHULI:c7/f26","TAB-FULL:v2p446#2/r5","ZAJJAJ:v1p196/r2","IBNABIHATIM:v1p210/r5","NAHHAS:v1p256/r16","THALABI:v1p261/r8","WAHIDI-BASIT:v3p252/r2","JISHUMI:v1p558/r10","KASHSHAF-FULL:v1p205/r9","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199#2/r4","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r7","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v1p199/r11","TABRISI:2:114#3/r3","TABRISI:2:114/r12","RAZI-FULL:v4p9/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:2:114#3/r4","NASAFI-FULL:v1p65#3/r12","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v1p571/r2","AB